<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mazandaran</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mazandaran is a province on the southern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its humid lowlands produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, which was a major export of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> took a particular interest in the province and its silk production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mazandaran.jpg" alt="Mazandaran" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Safa.daneshvar · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:030915-Damavand-Polour-IMG_3449-4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mazandaran lies on the southern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, in the Central Alborz mountains and the Hyrcanian forests. Its landscape runs from sandy beaches and plains to the snowcapped Alborz, which includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-damavand">Mount Damavand</a>. The name is Avestan and is understood as &quot;the gate of the giants&quot; or &quot;the valley of the giants&quot;. In the 10th-century Persian epic the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> it names a land of daevas and sorcerers that is difficult to conquer, and the epic also places the capital of the king Fereydun at Tamishe near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a>, in the land known as Tabarestan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human habitation in the area goes back at least 75,000 years. Caves at Belt and Hotu near Behshahr show people there about 9,000 years ago, and excavations at Gohar Tape in Rostamkola show that the area was urbanised more than 5,000 years ago. Before Iranian-speakers arrived, the native people were subsistence hunters and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herders. The Amard lived in the mountains bordering the Caspian, including the area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a>, and the Tapuri, a tribe among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, are mentioned by Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in the early seventh century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> stayed independent of it. Zoroastrian houses such as the Bavand and the Karen fought an effective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against the Umayyads. A short-lived Alid Shiite state collapsed before the Ziyarid princes took over. Later local dynasties fell into three groups: families of pre-Islamic origin, the Alid sayyids, and families of secondary importance.</p><p>After the Safavid period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> leader Agha Mohammad Khan campaigned south from Mazandaran and had incorporated it into his realm by 1782. On 21 March 1782 he proclaimed Sari his imperial capital. Local wars in the following years led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath Ali Shah</a> to move the capital from Sari to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mazandaran is one of the 31 provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, with its capital at Sari, and covers 24,842 square kilometres. The population is overwhelmingly Mazandarani, with minorities that include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilaks">Gilaks</a>, Azerbaijanis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>. Rice is present in almost every meal, and the coast is rich in seafood. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-iranian-railway">Trans-Iranian Railway</a>, built between 1927 and 1938 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a>, links <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. The Ramsar Convention on Wetlands was concluded in 1971 in the city of Ramsar in the province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mazandaran province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It made cultivation possible across an otherwise arid plateau and supported a largely sedentary peasantry growing wheat, barley, rice in the Caspian lowlands, and cash crops including cotton and, most importantly for Safavid commercial strategy, silk in the Caspian provinces of Gilan and <b>Mazandaran</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The clergy and the Qajar state reacted with violence: the Bábís fought desperate armed struggles in <b>Mazandaran</b>, Nayriz and Zanjan, and thousands were killed.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The sons of Kiya Afrasiyab murdered Fakhr al-Dawla Hasan, last ruler of the Bavand dynasty, in a bath, during a revolt of the nobles of <b>Mazandaran</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-17">17 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1349 he murdered Hasan and took control of Amol and much of <b>Mazandaran</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/afrasiyabids-of-mazandaran">Afrasiyabids of Mazandaran</a></li><li><blockquote>Sayyid Ali Kiya, a Zaydi sayyid supported by the Mar'ashi dynasty of <b>Mazandaran</b>, took Lahijan and eastern Gilan (Biyapish) around 1370.</blockquote><a href="/state/karkiya-dynasty">Karkiya dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Sayyid Qavam al-Din Mar'ashi, known as Mir-i Buzurg, was a Twelver Shia Sufi teacher whose dervish followers grew powerful in <b>Mazandaran</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/marashi-dynasty">Mar'ashi dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
